<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h3>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sequence compression is a ubiquitous operation in modern efficient large model architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen Team, 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI, 2026</a>)</cite>, and yet remains surprisingly underexplored. For example, block-wise sparse attention compresses each token block into a summary vector used to score and select relevant blocks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, while multimodal large language models (MLLMs) merge neighboring visual features into compact tokens before feeding them into the language backbone <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>. In both settings, the compressor acts as a critical information bottleneck. Once information is discarded, it cannot be recovered downstream.
One would expect compression to be a carefully designed architectural component. However, most models rely on simple mechanisms, such as parameter-free pooling, learned projections over concatenated tokens, or at best, attention-weighted pooling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We argue that these default compressors share a fundamental structural limitation, <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em>, <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">they aggregate tokens using weights that do not account for inter-token relationships</em>.
Mean pooling uses uniform weights, linear projection uses fixed position-dependent weights, and attention-weighted pooling scores each token independently.
As a result, redundant tokens can reinforce one another, while distinctive information is diluted.
From a statistical perspective, these methods primarily exploit <em id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">first-order</em> token statistics while ignoring <em id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">second-order</em> inter-token correlations, the very structure that reveals which information is redundant and which is distinctive.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a fundamentally different compression strategy. Rather than asking <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">which weighted average</em> best summarizes a set of tokens, we let the tokens define a regression problem
and take its <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fitted coefficients</em> as the summary. Specifically, our proposed TTR-Compressor, constructs a self-supervised ridge-regression problem directly from the input tokens, requiring no external labels, and uses its closed-form solution as the compressed representation.
This formulation creates a natural division: the outer training loop meta-learns <em id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">which</em> regression problem produces task-useful representations, while the inner closed-form solution determines <em id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how</em> that problem should be fitted to each input. The key distinction from conventional pooling and projection arises from the Gram matrix in the regression solution.
By explicitly capturing inter-token correlations, TTR-Compressor fits correlated tokens jointly, so redundant evidence has diminishing rather than purely additive influence.
This input-adaptive behavior cannot be reproduced by a fixed projection or additive token combination.
Moreover, learning the regularizer removes the need for ridge hyperparameter tuning in large-scale pretraining. Because the compression window is typically smaller than the channel dimension (<em id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em>, <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>), we solve the dual problem with an efficient <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times T</annotation></semantics></math> Cholesky factorization.
Thus, TTR-Compressor incurs minimal overhead and introduces only <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d</annotation></semantics></math> learnable parameters per compressor, making it an effective drop-in replacement for sequence compression.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate TTR-Compressor on two applications that impose fundamentally different requirements on compression. In <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">block-wise sparse attention</em>, the compressed summary must act as a faithful <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">retrieval key</em>. In this setting, TTR-Compressor consistently outperforms traditional baselines on RULER <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hsieh et al., 2024</a>)</cite> retrieval tasks, surpassing sophisticated attention-based compressors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2026</a>)</cite> by a considerable margin. In <em id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">visual token merging</em>, by contrast, the compressed summary must preserve a faithful <em id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">representation</em>. Here, TTR-Compressor consistently outperforms the widely used MLP merger across diverse compression ratios, and achieves comparable performance with 55.6% fewer visual tokens. Our contributions are summarized below:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A novel compression framework.</span> We introduce a test-time regression framework for sequence compression, reframing token summarization as an instance-adaptive optimization problem.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A practical operator.</span> We derive an efficient dual closed-form solution and eliminate hyperparameter tuning, making TTR-Compressor fully practical and scalable for large-scale pretraining.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical effectiveness.</span> As a drop-in component in large models, TTR-Compressor consistently outperforms conventional baselines in both block-wise sparse attention and visual token merging.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h3>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sequence compression.</span>
Compressing a sequence of tokens into a single vector is a fundamental operation across modern architectures, with applications ranging from multimodal token reduction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bolya et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cha et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to block-wise sparse attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lai et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Existing compression operators largely fall into three paradigms:
(1) <em id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">parameter-free pooling</em>, such as mean pooling;
(2) <em id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">concatenation followed by learned projection</em>; and
(3) <em id="S2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">attention-based compression</em>, which summarizes tokens via attention-weighted averaging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mohtashami &amp; Jaggi, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.
A fundamental limitation of these families is that they compute token combinations without explicitly accounting for the inter-token correlations.
In contrast, TTR-Compressor derives its representation as the solution to a linear system whose Gram matrix directly encodes these correlations, providing an expressive, drop-in replacement for the operators above.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Test-time regression and test-time training.</span>
The idea of adapting part of a network on each input at inference time dates back to fast-weight programmers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schmidhuber, 1992</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schlag et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and was revived as test-time training (TTT), first for robustness under distribution shift <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2020</a>)</cite> and then as sequence-modeling layers whose hidden states are small models updated by gradient descent on a self-supervised loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Behrouz et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Behrouz et al., 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. In-Place TTT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2026</a>)</cite> further extends this paradigm by directly adapting the existing MLP projection weights of pretrained Transformers. A parallel line of work reinterprets linear attention and its descendants (<em id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, DeltaNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Gated DeltaNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025c</a>)</cite>, Longhorn <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, Titans <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Behrouz et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>) as online regression: each layer maintains a key-to-value regressor that is refined with one gradient or delta-rule step per token and read out by the query. MesaNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">von Oswald et al., 2026</a>)</cite> pushes the inner optimization to convergence, solving the problem at every time step with conjugate gradients, while LaCT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026</a>)</cite> updates fast weights over large, unordered chunks to improve hardware utilization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">TTR-Compressor differs from these methods in two fundamental ways. First, because compression operates independently on small, disjoint blocks without cross-block dependencies, the regression admits an efficient, <em id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exact closed-form solution</em> via a single <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times T</annotation></semantics></math> dual solve. This avoids both iterative gradient-based inner optimization (as in TTT, ViT<sup id="S2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_sup">3</sup>, In-Place TTT, and MesaNet) and single-step approximations (such as delta-rule updates). Second, rather than using test-time regression to produce input-dependent model weights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bertinetto et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2020</a>)</cite> or a fast-weight memory that is subsequently queried <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schlag et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Behrouz et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>, we use regression to directly yield a <em id="S2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">representation</em> of a token block. Specifically, the fitted coefficients themselves serve as the compressed representation (<em id="S2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, a visual token or a retrieval key).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Joint Sequence Compression via Test-time Regression</h3>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce TTR-Compressor, a sequence compression operator based on <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">test-time regression</span>, as shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Compression as Test-time Regression ‣ 3 Joint Sequence Compression via Test-time Regression ‣ Rethinking Sequence Compression withTest-time Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(a). Given a sequence of token representations, TTR-Compressor constructs a regression problem from the tokens and uses its fitted coefficients as the compressed representation. We first present the regression formulation of compression and specify its construction, then derive an efficient solution and describe how TTR-Compressor is learned end-to-end within a model.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Compression as Test-time Regression</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formulate sequence compression as ridge regression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoerl &amp; Kennard, 1970</a>)</cite>. The tokens in a sequence provide regression observations, and the fitted regression coefficients serve as a compact representation of that sequence. The regression objective encourages this representation to capture the relationships among tokens, while regularization controls the magnitude of the fitted coefficients.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given a sequence <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{X}=[\bm{x}_{1},\ldots,\bm{x}_{T}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{X}=[\bm{x}_{1},\ldots,\bm{x}_{T}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, our goal is to compress its <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> token representations into a single vector <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We associate each token with a regression key <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{k}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{k}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and a scalar target <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, and define the compressed representation as:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}^{\star}=\arg\min_{\bm{h}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}\frac{1}{2}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\omega_{t}\big(\bm{k}_{t}^{\top}\bm{h}-v_{t}\big)^{2}+\frac{\lambda}{2}\|\bm{h}\|_{2}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒉</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝒉</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}^{\star}=\arg\min_{\bm{h}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}\frac{1}{2}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\omega_{t}\big(\bm{k}_{t}^{\top}\bm{h}-v_{t}\big)^{2}+\frac{\lambda}{2}\|\bm{h}\|_{2}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{t}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{t}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> determines the contribution of each token and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> controls regularization.
<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> summarizes the sequence by fitting the key-target relationships across its tokens.
The regression target <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S3.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">meta-learned</em> through the task loss, while the regression itself is constructed and solved for each input sequence during both training and inference.
Overall, we are solving a bi-level optimization, where the outer objective is the task loss that finds the task-useful target <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the inner objective is the test-time regression Eq. <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1 Compression as Test-time Regression ‣ 3 Joint Sequence Compression via Test-time Regression ‣ Rethinking Sequence Compression withTest-time Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> that solves the compressed representation <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <span id="S3.SS1.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">test-time</span> refers to fitting these input-specific coefficients; the learned model parameters remain fixed at inference.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.31574v1/method_v1.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/238;" width="548" height="238"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview of TTR-Compressor.</span> (a) Bilevel learning: The inner solve formulates sequence compression as test-time ridge regression with an exact dual Cholesky solve to obtain the representation <math id="S3.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, while the regression parameters are learned end-to-end via task gradients. (b) Input-dependent second-order adaptation: By jointly coupling tokens through the Gram matrix, TTR naturally diminishes the relative influence of redundant tokens compared to additive pooling. For intuitive illustration, we set <math id="S3.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{D}=\bm{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑫</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{D}=\bm{I}</annotation></semantics></math> and uniform targets <math id="S3.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{v}=\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{v}=\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math>; as typically learned in practice, a smaller regularizer <math id="S3.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{D}</annotation></semantics></math> further amplifies this deduplication effect, yielding an even higher relative share for distinct tokens.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Constructing the Regression Problem</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Keys and Targets.</span>
We construct regression keys by normalizing the input features and generate scalar targets using a learned projection:
<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{k}_{t}=\frac{\bm{x}_{t}}{\|\bm{x}_{t}\|_{2}},v_{t}=\bm{a}^{\top}\bm{x}_{t}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{k}_{t}=\frac{\bm{x}_{t}}{\|\bm{x}_{t}\|_{2}},v_{t}=\bm{a}^{\top}\bm{x}_{t},</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is shared across tokens and sequences within a compressor.
Key normalization ensures that <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{K}</annotation></semantics></math> measures directional (semantic) similarity alone <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2017</a>)</cite>. Crucially, this normalization is asymmetric, as targets retain original token magnitudes (<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}=\bm{a}^{\top}\bm{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}=\bm{a}^{\top}\bm{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>). If targets were also normalized (<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}=\bm{a}^{\top}\bm{k}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}=\bm{a}^{\top}\bm{k}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>), the fitting term would reduce to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\|\bm{K}(\bm{h}-\bm{a})\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\|\bm{K}(\bm{h}-\bm{a})\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, trivially collapsing <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> toward the sequence-invariant static vector <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}</annotation></semantics></math>. By preserving token norms in the targets, no constant vector can fit varying magnitudes, forcing <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> to dynamically integrate both token semantics and their relative importance.
The targets are derived entirely from the input, so fitting <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> requires no additional labels.
Optionally, targets can be further enriched by first applying a local convolution (1D for language and 2D for vision) to aggregate neighboring context before the projection, <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}=\bm{a}^{\top}\operatorname{Conv}(\bm{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Conv</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}=\bm{a}^{\top}\operatorname{Conv}(\bm{X})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Conv}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Conv</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Conv}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the convolution operator and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑿</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{X}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the local context window of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> neighboring tokens around position <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Alternative parameterizations of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are explored in the Appendix <a href="#A5.SS2" title="E.2 Ablation on the Value Parameterization ‣ Appendix E Additional Experimental Results ‣ Appendix ‣ Rethinking Sequence Compression withTest-time Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Uniform Token Contributions.</span>
We use uniform weights <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{t}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{t}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, assigning the same coefficient to every token’s regression error.
Let <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{K}=[\bm{k}_{1},\ldots,\bm{k}_{T}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{K}=[\bm{k}_{1},\ldots,\bm{k}_{T}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{v}=[v_{1},\ldots,v_{T}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{v}=[v_{1},\ldots,v_{T}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Setting the gradient of Eq. <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1 Compression as Test-time Regression ‣ 3 Joint Sequence Compression via Test-time Regression ‣ Rethinking Sequence Compression withTest-time Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> to zero gives us a closed-form solution:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{K}+\lambda\bm{I}_{d})\bm{h}^{\star}=\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{v},\qquad\bm{h}^{\star}=(\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{K}+\lambda\bm{I}_{d})^{-1}\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{v}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑲</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑰</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑲</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑰</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{K}+\lambda\bm{I}_{d})\bm{h}^{\star}=\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{v},\qquad\bm{h}^{\star}=(\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{K}+\lambda\bm{I}_{d})^{-1}\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{v}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the objective is strictly convex and has a unique solution.
The solution combines the key-target statistic <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{v}</annotation></semantics></math> with the sequence-dependent feature geometry <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{K}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learnable Diagonal Regularization.</span>

In conventional ridge regression, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is a tunable hyperparameter. Such tuning is expensive during large-model pretraining and complicates scaling to different model sizes. To avoid this, we replace <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\bm{I}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑰</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\bm{I}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> with a learnable positive diagonal matrix (meta-learned via the task loss), yielding a generalized ridge regression objective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tikhonov &amp; Arsenin, 1977</a>)</cite>:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{D}=\operatorname{diag}(\delta_{1},\ldots,\delta_{d}),\qquad\delta_{j}=\operatorname{softplus}(\rho_{j})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>softplus</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{D}=\operatorname{diag}(\delta_{1},\ldots,\delta_{d}),\qquad\delta_{j}=\operatorname{softplus}(\rho_{j}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\rho}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝆</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\rho}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is trainable.
The resulting objective and its solution are:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{h}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{h}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\arg\min_{\bm{h}}\frac{1}{2}\|\bm{K}\bm{h}-\bm{v}\|_{2}^{2}+\frac{1}{2}\bm{h}^{\top}\bm{D}\bm{h}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>𝒉</mi></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒉</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒉</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\arg\min_{\bm{h}}\frac{1}{2}\|\bm{K}\bm{h}-\bm{v}\|_{2}^{2}+\frac{1}{2}\bm{h}^{\top}\bm{D}\bm{h},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{K}+\bm{D})^{-1}\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{v}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑲</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝑫</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{K}+\bm{D})^{-1}\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{v},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">which solves a regularized linear system that takes the pairwise interaction of tokens into account.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Efficient Dual-form Solution</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Directly evaluating Eq. <a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.2 Constructing the Regression Problem ‣ 3 Joint Sequence Compression via Test-time Regression ‣ Rethinking Sequence Compression withTest-time Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> requires solving a <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times d</annotation></semantics></math> linear system.
When the number of tokens being compressed is typically smaller than the feature dimension, we instead use the equivalent dual form <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saunders et al., 1998</a>)</cite> in order to improve the computational efficiency:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}^{\star}=\bm{K}^{\top}(\bm{K}\bm{K}^{\top}+\lambda\bm{I}_{T})^{-1}\bm{v}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑰</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}^{\star}=\bm{K}^{\top}(\bm{K}\bm{K}^{\top}+\lambda\bm{I}_{T})^{-1}\bm{v}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This replaces the feature-space system with a <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times T</annotation></semantics></math> token-space system.
Its entries depend on pairwise similarities between regression keys, allowing the fitted representation to account for correlations among tokens.
We evaluate Eq. <a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.3 Efficient Dual-form Solution ‣ 3 Joint Sequence Compression via Test-time Regression ‣ Rethinking Sequence Compression withTest-time Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> by solving
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{K}\bm{K}^{\top}+\lambda\bm{I}_{T})\bm{\alpha}=\bm{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑰</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜶</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{K}\bm{K}^{\top}+\lambda\bm{I}_{T})\bm{\alpha}=\bm{v}</annotation></semantics></math>
and computing <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}^{\star}=\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜶</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}^{\star}=\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, without explicitly forming an inverse.
The resulting computational cost is
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(T^{2}d+T^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(T^{2}d+T^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>, compared with
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(Td^{2}+d^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(Td^{2}+d^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> for the direct primal formulation.
The dual form is therefore particularly suitable for compressing short sequences of high-dimensional features.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Obtaining an Efficient Dual Solution.</span>
With the learnable diagonal regularization introduced in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Constructing the Regression Problem ‣ 3 Joint Sequence Compression via Test-time Regression ‣ Rethinking Sequence Compression withTest-time Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\bm{I}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑰</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\bm{I}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is replaced by <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. To obtain an efficient dual solution, we rescale the keys as <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{\bm{K}}=\bm{K}\bm{D}^{-1/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>𝑲</mi><mi>𝑫</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\bm{K}}=\bm{K}\bm{D}^{-1/2}</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding the final form of TTR-Compressor:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{\operatorname{TTR}(\bm{X})=\bm{D}^{-1/2}\widetilde{\bm{K}}^{\top}\big(\bm{I}_{T}+\widetilde{\bm{K}}\widetilde{\bm{K}}^{\top}\big)^{-1}\bm{v}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mi>TTR</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>𝑫</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{\operatorname{TTR}(\bm{X})=\bm{D}^{-1/2}\widetilde{\bm{K}}^{\top}\big(\bm{I}_{T}+\widetilde{\bm{K}}\widetilde{\bm{K}}^{\top}\big)^{-1}\bm{v}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Because <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is diagonal, rescaling the keys costs only <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(Td)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(Td)</annotation></semantics></math>, preserving the dual formulation’s overall complexity.
In practice, we compute a Cholesky factorization
<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{L}\bm{L}^{\top}=\bm{I}_{T}+\widetilde{\bm{K}}\widetilde{\bm{K}}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑳</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑰</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{L}\bm{L}^{\top}=\bm{I}_{T}+\widetilde{\bm{K}}\widetilde{\bm{K}}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>,
solve <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{L}\bm{L}^{\top}\bm{\alpha}=\bm{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑳</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜶</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{L}\bm{L}^{\top}\bm{\alpha}=\bm{v}</annotation></semantics></math> through triangular solutions, and return
<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{D}^{-1/2}\widetilde{\bm{K}}^{\top}\bm{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑫</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>𝜶</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{D}^{-1/2}\widetilde{\bm{K}}^{\top}\bm{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Fully End-to-end Training</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The target projection <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and diagonal parameters <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> are optimized end-to-end through the differentiable closed-form solution using the original model’s training objective.
Each compressor maintains its own parameters, which are shared across all input sequences. At inference time, these learned parameters remain fixed, while <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> is recomputed independently for each input sequence.
TTR-Compressor therefore combines a learned regression objective with an exact, input-adaptive solution, without requiring iterative gradient-based optimization at test time.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Understanding TTR-Compressor</h3>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The learning process of TTR-Compressor can be decomposed into two distinct mechanisms: outer-loop training learns which regression problem is useful for compression, while the inner solution adapts the compressed representation to the current input’s statistics. We examine them separately.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Task-Supervised Learning of the Compression Objective</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The regression objective is not prescribed independently of the task. Its targets <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}=\bm{a}^{\top}\bm{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}=\bm{a}^{\top}\bm{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and regularization matrix <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{D}</annotation></semantics></math> are learned through the downstream use of the compressed representation. Writing <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}^{\star}_{\bm{a},\bm{\rho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝆</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}^{\star}_{\bm{a},\bm{\rho}}</annotation></semantics></math> for the regression solution, the training objective takes the form:</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\theta,\bm{a},\bm{\rho}}\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)}\left[\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{task}}\left(F_{\theta}\!\left(\bm{h}^{\star}_{\bm{a},\bm{\rho}}(\bm{X}_{\theta}(x))\right),y\right)\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝆</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>task</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝆</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\theta,\bm{a},\bm{\rho}}\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)}\left[\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{task}}\left(F_{\theta}\!\left(\bm{h}^{\star}_{\bm{a},\bm{\rho}}(\bm{X}_{\theta}(x))\right),y\right)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{X}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{X}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> produces the input representations and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the subsequent computation. Thus, the outer objective evaluates the usefulness of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> for the final task, rather than leveraging its learnability to merely minimize the inner regression error.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learning the Targets and Regularization.</span>
This coupling can be seen directly from the gradients.
Let
<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{A}=\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{K}+\bm{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑲</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝑫</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{A}=\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{K}+\bm{D}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{g}=\nabla_{\bm{h}^{\star}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{task}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒈</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{g}=\nabla_{\bm{h}^{\star}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{task}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}=\bm{A}^{-1}\bm{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒈</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}=\bm{A}^{-1}\bm{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Differentiating the regression solution gives:</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nabla_{\bm{a}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{task}}=\bm{X}^{\top}\bm{K}\bm{s},\qquad\nabla_{\bm{\rho}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{task}}=-\operatorname{sigmoid}(\bm{\rho})\odot\bm{h}^{\star}\odot\bm{s}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>task</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>𝑲</mi><mi>𝒔</mi><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝝆</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>task</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>sigmoid</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝆</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\bm{a}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{task}}=\bm{X}^{\top}\bm{K}\bm{s},\qquad\nabla_{\bm{\rho}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{task}}=-\operatorname{sigmoid}(\bm{\rho})\odot\bm{h}^{\star}\odot\bm{s},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\odot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\odot</annotation></semantics></math> denotes element-wise multiplication. The downstream gradient therefore shapes both the scalar targets derived from tokens via <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and the coordinate-wise penalties imposed through <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, the scalar targets require no predefined semantics; instead, they serve as learned measurements whose fitted coefficients yield informative representations for downstream layers. In this sense, the regression provides a structural inductive bias for compression, while outer-loop optimization steers this structural bias towards better handling the downstream task.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Input-Dependent Second-Order Adaptation</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond learning the regression objective, the inner solve adapts the
compressed representation to the correlation structure of each input.
For isotropic regularization, the dual-form solution is</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}^{\star}=\bm{K}^{\top}(\bm{K}\bm{K}^{\top}+\lambda\bm{I})^{-1}\bm{v}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}^{\star}=\bm{K}^{\top}(\bm{K}\bm{K}^{\top}+\lambda\bm{I})^{-1}\bm{v}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Let
<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{G}=\bm{K}\bm{K}^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times T}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝑲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{G}=\bm{K}\bm{K}^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times T}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Since the keys are normalized, each entry
<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{ij}=\bm{k}_{i}^{\top}\bm{k}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{ij}=\bm{k}_{i}^{\top}\bm{k}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> directly measures the similarity between
tokens <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{G}</annotation></semantics></math> captures the input-dependent second-order correlation
structure of the token group.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The compressed representation can equivalently be written as</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}^{\star}=\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{\alpha},\qquad\bm{\alpha}=(\bm{G}+\lambda\bm{I})^{-1}\bm{v}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜶</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}^{\star}=\bm{K}^{\top}\bm{\alpha},\qquad\bm{\alpha}=(\bm{G}+\lambda\bm{I})^{-1}\bm{v}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Unlike pooling and projection, where token contributions are aggregated independently,
the effective weights <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜶</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> are determined jointly by the target
responses and all pairwise token correlations.
Highly similar tokens are explicitly coupled through the Gram matrix,
so redundant evidence exhibits diminishing rather than additive
influence.
Conversely, information not already explained by strongly
correlated tokens can retain a larger relative influence when supported
by the regression targets, as shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Compression as Test-time Regression ‣ 3 Joint Sequence Compression via Test-time Regression ‣ Rethinking Sequence Compression withTest-time Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(b).
TTR therefore adapts its compression rule to the second-order structure
of every input rather than applying a fixed or independently weighted
combination of tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learning the inter-token geometry.</span>
With the learnable diagonal regularizer <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, this interpretation
extends to the rescaled keys
<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{\bm{K}}=\bm{K}\bm{D}^{-1/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>𝑲</mi><mi>𝑫</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\bm{K}}=\bm{K}\bm{D}^{-1/2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The corresponding token Gram matrix becomes</p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\bm{G}}=\widetilde{\bm{K}}\widetilde{\bm{K}}^{\top}=\bm{K}\bm{D}^{-1}\bm{K}^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝑲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑫</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\bm{G}}=\widetilde{\bm{K}}\widetilde{\bm{K}}^{\top}=\bm{K}\bm{D}^{-1}\bm{K}^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Training therefore learns which feature dimensions should matter more
when measuring token similarity.
The outer loop learns a task-adapted correlation geometry, while the
inner solve adapts the compressed representation to the correlation
structure of each individual input.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Applications and Experimental Results</h3>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate TTR-Compressor in two settings: block-wise sparse attention for efficient language modeling, where compressed representations guide context selection, and visual token merging for efficient multimodal understanding, where they serve as the visual input to the language backbone.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Block-wise Sparse Attention</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Architecture and Baselines.</span>
Block-wise sparse attention partitions the context into blocks, scores their compressed representations against each query, and attends to tokens in selected blocks alongside a local window. The compressor therefore determines which distant information remains accessible: an uninformative summary can prevent a relevant block from being selected, regardless of the quality of its individual token representations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt HiLS-Attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2026</a>)</cite> as a base framework and vary only the compressor. HiLS-Attention has three effective architectural components: hierarchical softmax attention, which combines within-block attention with learned block-level weights; low-rank query calibration, which adapts queries for scoring compressed representations; and HoPE, which applies RoPE to only a subset of feature dimensions to support length extrapolation. We compare four compressors: mean pooling, concat &amp; projection, the attention-weighted pooling used in the original HiLS-Attention, and TTR-Compressor. Full attention and sliding-window attention serve as additional references.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training and Evaluation.</span>
We train 345M-parameter language models on the FineWeb dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Penedo et al., 2024</a>)</cite> with an 8K context length. Training runs for 30,000 optimization steps with a global batch size of 128 sequences, corresponding to approximately 30B tokens, and uses AdamW with a learning rate of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. All sparse attention variants use 64-token blocks, select up to 32 distant blocks per query, and retain a 512-token local window.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluation covers two complementary aspects. First, since queries use the compressed representations to retrieve relevant blocks, retrieval accuracy directly reflects compression quality. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hatamizadeh et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, we evaluate on the single-needle (S-NIAH) and multi-key (MK-NIAH) retrieval tasks from RULER <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hsieh et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which require locating relevant information among distractors, as the most direct and demanding test of the compressor. We evaluate at 4K–32K context lengths, including extrapolation beyond the 8K training length. Additionally, LAMBADA accuracy and the zero-shot benchmarks in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1 Block-wise Sparse Attention ‣ 5 Applications and Experimental Results ‣ Rethinking Sequence Compression withTest-time Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> assess contextual prediction, commonsense reasoning, and question answering.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S5.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:397.5pt;height:62.6pt;vertical-align:-29.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-145.4pt,22.9pt) scale(0.577531802622268,0.577531802622268) ;">
<table id="S5.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
