<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span> As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1 Block-wise Sparse Attention ‣ 5 Applications and Experimental Results ‣ Rethinking Sequence Compression withTest-time Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the choice of compressor largely determines retrieval quality. TTR-Compressor outperforms all baselines by a large margin, and its advantage persists when extrapolating to 16K and 32K. We attribute this to its second-order modeling: pooling and projection add up tokens independently, so the redundant context that fills most of a block drowns out the few informative tokens. The Gram matrix in TTR-Compressor fits correlated tokens jointly and reduces the influence of redundant evidence, so the resulting key keeps the distinctive content that queries need to find. The convolutional variant brings a further gain, reaching 12.24 points over the strongest baseline, as convolution makes the regression targets more informative and thus improves compression quality. On the benchmarks in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1 Block-wise Sparse Attention ‣ 5 Applications and Experimental Results ‣ Rethinking Sequence Compression withTest-time Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the evaluation contexts are too short to activate sparse attention, so these results only reflect the common-sense reasoning ability acquired during pretraining. Concat &amp; Projection has substantially more parameters than the other compressors, which may benefit pretraining. TTR-Compressor performs well and on par with full attention.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Visual Token Merger</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Architecture and Baselines.</span>
High-resolution images produce many visual tokens, making their direct processing by a language backbone expensive. Modern multimodal models therefore commonly use a spatial merger to reduce the visual sequence length. A widely adopted design concatenates each <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> group of neighboring visual features and maps it to one token through a two-layer MLP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen Team, 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>. Longer multimodal contexts and agentic workflows with repeated visual observations further motivate stronger compression, such as <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> merging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI, 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We compare TTR-Compressor with the standard MLP merger. TTR-Compressor fits one regression per spatial group to compress its four or nine visual features into a single vector, which a two-layer MLP then projects to the language model’s hidden dimension. Its convolutional variant applies a <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> depthwise convolution to the feature map before constructing the regression targets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training and Evaluation.</span>
We use the ViT of Qwen3-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2025</a>)</cite> as the vision encoder and Qwen3-4B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> as the language model. Training consists of two stages. The first stage trains only the merger modules on LLaVA-558K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> for one epoch with a learning rate of <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>. The second stage jointly trains the merger modules and language backbone on 1.3M instruction samples mixing image <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, multi-image <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and video <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> data for one epoch with a learning rate of <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate on 4 types of benchmarks: general visual question answering (MMBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024c</a>)</cite>, MMStar <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, RealWorldQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">xAI, 2024</a>)</cite>), knowledge-intensive multimodal reasoning (MMMU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, AI2D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kembhavi et al., 2016</a>)</cite>), text, infographic, and chart understanding (OCRBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024d</a>)</cite>, InfoVQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tito et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, ChartQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Masry et al., 2022</a>)</cite>), and spatial reasoning (MMSI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, ERQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2025</a>)</cite>).</p>
</div>
