<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span> As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2 Visual Token Merger ‣ 5 Applications and Experimental Results ‣ Rethinking Sequence Compression withTest-time Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, TTR-Compressor outperforms the MLP merger under both compression ratios. Under <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> merging, it raises the average from <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="54.93" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>54.93</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">54.93</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="55.92" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>55.92</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">55.92</annotation></semantics></math>, and its convolutional variant further improves it to <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="56.03" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>56.03</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">56.03</annotation></semantics></math>. The advantage grows under stronger <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> merging. The gains are most pronounced on text-rich benchmarks, where fine-grained details are easily lost during merging: under <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> merging, the convolutional variant improves over the MLP merger by <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.10</annotation></semantics></math> on OCRBench, <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.54" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.54</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.54</annotation></semantics></math> on InfoVQA, and <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.56" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.56</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.56</annotation></semantics></math> on ChartQA. On average, it outperforms the MLP merger by <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.58" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.58</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.58</annotation></semantics></math> points. Notably, TTR-Compressor (w/ Conv) with <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> merging achieves an average of <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="54.85" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>54.85</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">54.85</annotation></semantics></math>, close to the <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="54.93" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>54.93</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">54.93</annotation></semantics></math> of the MLP merger with <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> merging, while using fewer than half as many visual tokens.
We attribute the strong performance of TTR-Compressor to its second-order modeling. The MLP merger concatenates the features in each group and maps them with fixed weights, so the mixing does not depend on how the features relate to each other. When most features in a group are redundant, such as background patches, they dominate the merged token and wash out fine-grained details. In contrast, TTR-Compressor fits correlated features jointly and reduces the influence of redundant ones, so the merged token keeps the distinctive content within the group.</p>
</div>
