<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theoretical Complexity and Parameter Efficiency.</span>
For a sequence <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> compressed into <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.3 Efficiency Analysis ‣ 5 Applications and Experimental Results ‣ Rethinking Sequence Compression withTest-time Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> summarizes the asymptotic complexity and parameter count across different paradigms. Under the targeted regime where <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\ll d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\ll d</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\{4,9,64\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\{4,9,64\}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 1024</annotation></semantics></math>), TTR-Compressor fundamentally avoids the <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(d^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(d^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> overhead and delivers strong, data-dependent expressiveness with only <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d</annotation></semantics></math> parameters.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:55%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.31574v1/wall_clock_time.svg" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:307/115;" width="307" height="115"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Wall-clock training time comparisons.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical Training Efficiency.</span>
Following the setups in Sections <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Block-wise Sparse Attention ‣ 5 Applications and Experimental Results ‣ Rethinking Sequence Compression withTest-time Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> and <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Visual Token Merger ‣ 5 Applications and Experimental Results ‣ Rethinking Sequence Compression withTest-time Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.3 Efficiency Analysis ‣ 5 Applications and Experimental Results ‣ Rethinking Sequence Compression withTest-time Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> benchmarks the actual wall-clock training efficiency. For block-wise sparse attention (<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8\times\text{H100}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>H100</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8\times\text{H100}</annotation></semantics></math> GPUs), while mean pooling is the most efficient, its representational capacity is inherently limited; other compression variants exhibit comparable training times. For visual token merging (<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 8\times\text{H100}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>H100</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 8\times\text{H100}</annotation></semantics></math> GPUs), TTR-Compressor achieves efficiency on par with the widely used MLP. Notably, while marginally slower under <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> merging, TTR-Compressor outpaces MLP under <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> merging, highlighting its superior efficiency and scalability at higher compression ratios.</p>
</div>
</section>
