<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Criticality of Key Normalization.</span>
As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 5.4 Ablation Study and Analysis ‣ 5 Applications and Experimental Results ‣ Rethinking Sequence Compression withTest-time Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we find that omitting key normalization (<math id="S5.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}_{t}=\mathbf{x}_{t}/\|\mathbf{x}_{t}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}_{t}=\mathbf{x}_{t}/\|\mathbf{x}_{t}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) precipitates a collapse across retrieval tasks. We attribute this to two reasons.
First, from an optimization perspective, since scalar targets are linearly generated via <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}=\mathbf{a}^{\top}\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}=\mathbf{a}^{\top}\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, using raw keys (<math id="S5.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}_{t}=\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}_{t}=\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>) makes the static weight <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}</annotation></semantics></math> a trivial exact fit (<math id="S5.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}_{t}^{\top}\mathbf{a}\equiv v_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐚</mi></mrow><mo>≡</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}_{t}^{\top}\mathbf{a}\equiv v_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>).
Consequently, the regularized solution <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> is biased toward the fixed vector <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, which weakens the instance adaptivity of test-time regression and pushes it toward an input-invariant projection.
Second, from a geometric and numerical viewpoint, outlier tokens with large norms dominate the Gram matrix <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}\mathbf{K}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐊𝐊</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}\mathbf{K}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, causing correlation modeling to reflect token norms rather than semantic similarity.
Normalization resolves both issues at once: it breaks the trivial alignment with static parameters and prevents large-norm tokens from dominating the Gram matrix.</p>
