<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">From Static Heuristics to Anisotropic Regularization.</span>
Table <a href="#S5.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 5.4 Ablation Study and Analysis ‣ 5 Applications and Experimental Results ‣ Rethinking Sequence Compression withTest-time Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> compares fixed regularization scalars against learnable formulations. All fixed settings outperform the baseline. Moderate values around <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math> perform well, while performance decreases when <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is too small (<math id="S5.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.4364" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.4364</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.4364</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>) or too large (<math id="S5.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.4238" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.4238</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.4238</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math>).
Notably, a <em id="S5.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Learnable Scalar</em> already achieves a competitive average accuracy of <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.4971" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.4971</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.4971</annotation></semantics></math>, demonstrating that the outer-loop task objective can reliably discover effective regularization strengths end-to-end.
Extending this to a coordinate-wise <em id="S5.SS4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Learnable Diagonal</em> (<math id="S5.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}=\mathrm{diag}(\mathrm{softplus}(\bm{\rho}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>softplus</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}=\mathrm{diag}(\mathrm{softplus}(\bm{\rho}))</annotation></semantics></math>) achieves the overall best performance. This progression shows the benefit of learning a separate regularization strength for each channel: the model penalizes less useful feature directions more strongly while preserving task-critical ones.</p>
</div>
